# Drafted by Gemini (R15 hook), edited.

~ adela_start
Narrator: The midnight bells have gone quiet in Founder's Round, and a woman in a heavy fur coat and low-brimmed hat beckons from the shadow of the stone statue.
cutscene dc_adela_arm
Narrator: By the guttering tallow of a hooded lantern, her trembling fingers peel back a long woollen glove.
cutscene dc_adela_arm_2
Narrator: The forearm beneath is the colour of wet ash, withered nearly to the bone from wrist to elbow, with the fingers curled in tight.
if flag.act2_done >= 1
Narrator: The creeping grey has climbed well past the joint now, drinking the life from her upper arm.
endif
cutscene end
adela_druskenvald [afraid]: Forgive the dreadful theatrics, darlings, truly, I am at my wits' end. Please keep your voices down and tell me you can help a foolish woman undo a bargain.
* Ask what happened to her arm -> adela_deal
* Ask about the prophecy that frightened her -> adela_prophecy
* Ask what she is offering in return -> adela_pay
* We will go down to the bayou -> adela_accept
* Not right now -> adela_later

~ adela_deal
adela_druskenvald [sad]: Gaston Doray filled my head with tales of a gentleman dealmaker down in the Drowned Crossroads. I crept into the swamp and traded my wedding band for a promise that Phillip's old strength would return when we must leave.
adela_druskenvald [afraid]: Now the road has vanished from my memory altogether, and every morning this dreadful grey creeps another inch up my arm.
-> adela_start

~ adela_prophecy
adela_druskenvald [weary]: I had the most ghastly waking dream of the Crooked Moon splitting the sky in a rage. It showed me that my husband and I would only ever leave this valley upon a road of drifting ash.
-> adela_start

~ adela_pay
adela_druskenvald [neutral]: I can give you one hundred and fifty gold pieces each from my private pin-money. There will be fifty more apiece if my husband never hears a breath of my indiscretion.
-> adela_start

~ adela_accept
adela_druskenvald [afraid]: Oh, bless you. Only promise me one thing before you set out: Phillip carries quite enough sorrow, and he must not know what I have done.
* We will keep your counsel -> adela_secret
* We make no promises about Phillip -> adela_open

~ adela_secret
adela_druskenvald [smile]: You have the gratitude of a very tired lady, my dears. Look for the gentleman in the good coat where the black water pools, and bring my ring home.
set adela_secret_kept
set crossroads_asked
quest withered_arm asked
Narrator: She slips back into the mist toward the manor, leaving you with the damp wind rising off the south road, where the valley sinks into the bayou.
-> END

~ adela_open
adela_druskenvald [weary]: Fair enough, I suppose a beggar in borrowed wool can hardly dictate terms. Just bring my ring back from those black waters before there is nothing left of me to scold.
set crossroads_asked
quest withered_arm asked
Narrator: She lifts her chin with practiced grace and melts into the fog, leaving the south road open before you toward the dark water at the valley's floor.
-> END

~ adela_later
adela_druskenvald [sad]: I see. Do not take too long to find your charity, darlings, or there will be very little of me left to hire you.
Narrator: She draws the long woollen glove back over the brittle grey bone and retreats into the shadow of the plinth.
-> END

~ adela_hex
Narrator: Lady Adela steps out from behind a colonnade to bar your path, her breath pluming in the chill air and her left sleeve hanging frighteningly hollow.
adela_druskenvald [weary]: I saw you ride back through the gates, and I could not wait another hour in the dark. The rot has passed my elbow now, creeping toward the shoulder, and the cold never stops.
set adela_hex_seen
* Ask how long she has left -> hex_arm
* We are going now -> END

~ hex_arm
adela_druskenvald [afraid]: I cannot count the days when there is no sun to measure them by, my dears. All I know is that the ring's absence sits like an icy draught inside my very bones.
-> END
